{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 0, "result": {"protocolVersion": "2025-06-18", "capabilities": {"tools": {"listChanged": false}, "resources": {}, "prompts": {}}, "serverInfo": {"name": "ucas", "title": "ucas, a computer algebra system for programmers", "version": "1"}, "instructions": "ucas analyses functions (exhaustively on machine types), does exact algebra and calculus, transforms, number theory and finite fields, and finds cheaper implementations, each answer checked or proved.  Expressions are ucas syntax strings; give parameters types (x: u8, x: f32, x: real) where it matters.  Definitions persist across calls.  An ambiguous input is an error listing its readings: retry with args.assume.KEY.  structuredContent holds the typed result, checks and proof."}}
{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 1, "result": {"prompts": [{"name": "cheaper_implementation", "description": "Find and prove a cheaper implementation of a function on its machine types", "arguments": [{"name": "f", "description": "the function, with types: \"days(m: u8 in 1..12) = match m { 2 => 28, 4 | 6 | 9 | 11 => 30, _ => 31 }\" or isqrt(x: u4)", "required": true}]}, {"name": "prove_identity", "description": "Prove (or refute with a counterexample) that two expressions are equal", "arguments": [{"name": "identity", "description": "the claimed identity, e.g. sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^2 = 1 or (x: u8) * 2 = x << 1", "required": true}]}, {"name": "explore_function", "description": "Study a function: its properties, critical points, integral and a plot", "arguments": [{"name": "f", "description": "the function, e.g. x^3 - 3x or sin(x)/x", "required": true}]}]}}
{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 2, "result": {"prompts": [{"name": "cheaper_implementation", "description": "Find and prove a cheaper implementation of a function on its machine types", "arguments": [{"name": "f", "description": "the function, with types: \"days(m: u8 in 1..12) = match m { 2 => 28, 4 | 6 | 9 | 11 => 30, _ => 31 }\" or isqrt(x: u4)", "required": true}]}, {"name": "prove_identity", "description": "Prove (or refute with a counterexample) that two expressions are equal", "arguments": [{"name": "identity", "description": "the claimed identity, e.g. sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^2 = 1 or (x: u8) * 2 = x << 1", "required": true}]}, {"name": "explore_function", "description": "Study a function: its properties, critical points, integral and a plot", "arguments": [{"name": "f", "description": "the function, e.g. x^3 - 3x or sin(x)/x", "required": true}]}]}}
{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 3, "result": {"description": "Prove (or refute with a counterexample) that two expressions are equal", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": {"type": "text", "text": "Decide whether (x+y)^2 = x^2+2*x*y+y^2 holds, with the ucas tools.  For polynomial or rational expressions use algebra simplify on the difference (an exact normal form is a proof; note any conditions where a factor cancelled).  For machine-typed expressions use analyze equiv (exhaustive over the domain, or a counterexample).  For trigonometric ones use complex trig, which checks each form.  State which kind of proof you got (symbolic, exhaustive, numeric)."}}]}}
{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 4, "result": {"description": "Find and prove a cheaper implementation of a function on its machine types", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": {"type": "text", "text": "Use the ucas tools to find a cheaper implementation of days(m: u8 in 1..12) = m.  Define it with session/define (typed parameters), then: analyze range and wrap (what it computes, where it overflows), analyze cost (under the active GPU model), analyze find (cheaper equivalent implementations, verified on the whole domain) and, for up to 6 input bits, analyze gates (a minimal gate network with a proof).  Confirm the best candidate with analyze equiv and report it with its cost and the proof each tool gave."}}]}}
{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 5, "result": {"description": "Study a function: its properties, critical points, integral and a plot", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": {"type": "text", "text": "Explore sin(x)/x with the ucas tools: properties identify, analyze crit and range, calculus diff and integrate, properties asymptotes if it is rational, and plot plot (the picture comes back as an image).  Summarise what is proved and what is numeric."}}]}}
